course: arts
lesson: 22
title: How films are made: script, crew, sets, editing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A film starts as a script, the written plan of every scene and every line, and then hundreds of people turn that paper into pictures and sound.
explain: The crew are the people behind the camera: the director decides how it should look and feel, camera and sound teams record it, set builders and costume makers create the world, and the editor puts it all together.
explain: Films are shot like a jigsaw, not like a story: all the scenes in one place are filmed together, even if one is at the start and one at the end, and the editor puts the pieces in order later.
explain: Picture a school film on a tablet: one child writes, one films, one acts and one edits, and it works better than one child trying to be everything at once.
explain: People talk about it in their own words: "wack" means bad or weird, "extra" means over the top, and in London "innit" just means "isn't it?" or "right?".
fact: A big film can need hundreds or even thousands of people, and the credits at the end list them all.
fact: Films are usually filmed out of order to save time and money, for example shooting every scene in one place together.
fact: The editor joins the shots together in the right order after filming.
fact: A clapperboard shows the scene and take number, and its clap helps the editor match the sound to the picture, which are often recorded separately.
fact: A take is one try at filming a shot; directors often film many takes to get the acting, camera and light right together.
fact: A plane flying over or a camera wobble can mean another take, so many takes do not mean bad acting.
fact: Many films are adaptations of books, comics or games, and people argue whether the book or the film is better.
fact: Film sets are built to look real only from where the camera points; behind, they are often bare wood.
fact: A cut is a change from one shot to the next; studies of Hollywood films report that shots have got shorter over the decades.
fact: Films often use one city to pretend to be another, because filming there is cheaper or easier, and the crew change street signs so it looks like the right city.
fact: Silent films, from before about 1927, had no recorded talking, so actors used big faces and movements; words were shown on cards and music was often played live in the cinema.
exercise: Why do film credits go on for so long? | Hundreds of people worked on it and are named | The cinema wants everyone to leave slowly so the doors do not jam | Every actor's whole family is listed too, as a thank-you | Names repeat to fill time
exercise: Ananya hears the ending may be filmed first. Why are films shot out of order? | Scenes in one place are filmed together to save money | Directors like to confuse the actors so they look surprised on screen | The ending is always the easiest scene, so it is done first to warm up | The law says shoot backwards
exercise: What is the clapperboard's clap for? | It helps match the sound to the picture | It tells the audience in the cinema that a new scene has started | It wakes the actors and crew up between long scenes on set | It scares away birds and dogs from the set before filming
exercise: An actor says a scene took 40 takes. What does that most likely mean? | Many things had to go right at once | She forgot every single one of her lines forty times in a row | The camera was broken for forty days and had to be fixed each time | The director was asleep
exercise: What is a take? | One try at filming a shot | The money a film earns | A list of actors in a scene | A cup of tea for the crew
exercise: Matt's mate says, "The book is always better." What is true? | It is argued: some prefer the book, some the film | Every film ever made is worse than the book it came from | Films based on books are not allowed to change a single thing | Films are always better
exercise: Rosie sees the spaceship is a painted wooden box. Why do sets look real on screen? | They only have to look right from the camera | Painted wood turns shiny like metal under the bright studio lights | The audience in the cinema is too far away to see anything properly | Real old spaceships are lent to film studios by space agencies
exercise: Daniel's grandad says old films did not "jump about". What has changed? | Shots got shorter, so films cut faster | Old films had no cameras at all and were acted on stage instead | Modern films are never edited, so they jump about by themselves | Old films were never cut
exercise: Hannah's class makes a film. Why can't Jack be star, director and camera person? | Films work better with roles shared across a team | It is against the rules for kids to act | A director is never allowed to hold a camera | Tablets only let one job be done at a time
exercise: Ravi's dad says a London car chase was filmed in another city. Why do films do that? | It can be cheaper or easier elsewhere | Filming anything in London has been banned by law for many years | Cars are not allowed to drive fast on any of the roads in London | The real city was too small
exercise: Why do actors in old silent films act "so extra"? | With no talking, faces and bodies showed the feelings | Actors in the past did not know how to act properly or calmly | The cameras back then could only see very big, slow movements | They were all shouting
exercise: What did people hear in a silent film cinema? | Music often played live in the cinema | Total silence the whole time | Actors speaking from behind the screen | Recorded voices on a disc
exercise: Who puts the shots together in the right order? | The editor | The costume maker | The caterer | The ticket seller
exercise: What is a script? | The written plan of the scenes and lines | The money the film costs | The poster for the film | A machine that records sound
exercise: What is an adaptation? | A film based on a book, comic or game | A film shot in only one take | A film with no music | A film made by one person
test: Your cousin goes, "The director and the stars make the whole film, sorted." What is the mistake? | A film needs a huge crew | He is right, nobody else is needed apart from the stars | Only the camera operator really matters, the rest just watch | Films mostly make themselves with computers these days
test: What would happen if a plane flew over during a quiet love scene? | They would probably film another take | The plane would be kept in the film | The film would be cancelled | The actors would have to stop for a year
test: A cartoon shows a film crew filming the story in perfect order from page one. What is usually different in real life? | Films are shot in a puzzle order and edited later | Real films are always filmed backwards, starting from the very end | Real films are filmed all in one day so the actors do not get older | Real films skip the script
test: Kid in Texas says, "Bro, the set was mid, it's just boards at the back" ("mid" means not great). What is the best reply? | Sets only need to look real to the camera | Real films always use real buildings, so this one was a cheap film | The film crew simply forgot to finish building the back of the set | Boards look better on film
test: Which statement about books and their films is fair? | It is argued which is better | Books are proven by scientists to be better every single time | Films are proven by scientists to be better every single time | Nobody has ever made a film from a book, they are always new
test: Granda in Belfast says, "Them old films had no sound at all, so they hadn't." What is the correction? | Live music was often played in the cinema | He is right, the cinema was silent | The actors' voices were played on a radio | Audiences had to hum the music themselves
test: Your class film has a great shot of a dog, then a shot of a bowl. Who decides which comes first? | The editor | The dog decides, by where it walks while the camera is filming | The tablet decides automatically, in the order the shots were taken | Nobody decides, it is chosen by chance when the film is saved
test: Why might a film set in New York be filmed in another city? | It is cheaper or easier, and signs are changed | New York does not allow cameras of any kind in its streets | Other cities look exactly like New York, so nobody can ever tell | The actors live there
test: An editor says, "Snap, then we can sync it" ("sync" means match up). What is she using? | A clapperboard to match sound and picture | A camera flash to light the scene | A script to check the lines | A microphone to record music
test: Why do many films today cut every few seconds? | Shot lengths have become shorter over the years | Cameras now stop by themselves every few seconds | Editors are paid for every cut they make | Audiences used to complain films were too fast
test: Which is true about forty takes of one scene? | It is normal on a film set | It proves that the actor is terrible and should be replaced | It is against film rules, so the director will be told off | It only ever happens in cartoons, never in films with real actors
exam: Identify the principal reason for which motion pictures are customarily filmed in a sequence other than that of the narrative. | Scenes sharing a location are recorded together for economy | Directors are obliged by statute to film every narrative in reverse order | The concluding scene is invariably the least demanding and is therefore filmed first | To confuse the performers
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the function of the clapperboard? | It permits the subsequent synchronisation of sound and picture | It signals to the audience that a scene has commenced | It serves solely as an ornament of tradition | It records the dialogue of the performers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning silent films. | Feeling was shown by gesture, with live music | No sound of any description accompanied their exhibition in any cinema | Recorded speech was reproduced from gramophone discs during exhibition | Actors spoke very loudly
exam: Ascertain the person principally responsible for the assembly of filmed shots into their final order. | The editor | The caterer | The costume maker | The member of the audience
